---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const proofCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Given: AB = AC', note: 'An isosceles triangle: two equal sides. To show: the base angles ∠ABC and ∠ACB are equal.' },
    { math: '2 · Bisect the apex angle', note: 'Draw AD splitting ∠BAC into two equal halves. Every angle has a bisector — Euclid, Proposition 9.' },
    { math: '3 · △ABD ≅ △ACD (SAS)', note: 'AB = AC (given), ∠BAD = ∠CAD (construction), AD = AD (shared side). Side–angle–side congruence.' },
    { math: '4 · ∴ ∠B = ∠C', note: 'Congruent triangles have equal matching angles. Two facts from one construction — the Euclidean method in miniature.' },
  ],
};

const geoProofSort = {
  "prompt": "File each guarantee under the method that provides it:",
  "buckets": [
    "a measurement gives",
    "a proof gives"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "the answer for the one triangle you checked",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "an accuracy limited by your instruments",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the result for every triangle at once, including ones too skewed to draw",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "every step visible and checkable",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "The leap from 'the ones I checked' to 'all of them' is what makes mathematics different from every empirical science."
};

const geoAngleMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each angle fact with what it sums to (or claims):",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "angles on a straight line",
      "right": "sum to 180°"
    },
    {
      "left": "angles around a point",
      "right": "sum to 360°"
    },
    {
      "left": "complementary angles",
      "right": "sum to 90°"
    },
    {
      "left": "vertically opposite angles",
      "right": "are equal"
    }
  ],
  "why": "A full turn is 360° — a convention inherited from Babylonian base-60 astronomy."
};

const geoParallelCloze = {
  "text": "Parallel lines cut by a transversal: corresponding angles are equal (the %%0%%-shape), alternate angles are equal (the %%1%%-shape), and co-interior angles sum to %%2%% (the C-shape).",
  "answers": [
    [
      "F",
      "f"
    ],
    [
      "Z",
      "z"
    ],
    [
      "180°",
      "180"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Two letters that look like the angle patterns, and the straight-line total."
};

const geoPolyEst = {
  "prompt": "The interior angles of an n-sided polygon sum to (n − 2) × 180°. Roughly what is the sum for a hexagon?",
  "unit": "°",
  "answer": 720,
  "factor": 1.2,
  "why": "(6 − 2) × 180° = 720° — 180° for a triangle, 360° for a quadrilateral, 540° for a pentagon, 720° for a hexagon.",
  "context": "draw a line through one corner parallel to the opposite side and the triangle's angles rearrange into a straight line"
};

const geoAssBranch = {
  "root": "Two triangles share two sides and a non-included angle (ASS). Are they congruent?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "What is the verdict?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Yes — three facts always pin a triangle down",
          "next": "trap",
          "feedback": "Not these three: ASS famously does not work — two different triangles can share two sides and a non-included angle."
        },
        {
          "label": "No — only SSS, SAS and ASA (plus RHS) guarantee congruence",
          "next": "works",
          "feedback": "Right: the included angle is what pins the third side down; a non-included angle leaves the shape ambiguous."
        },
        {
          "label": "Yes, if the shared angle is a right angle",
          "next": "rhs",
          "feedback": "That is RHS — right angle, hypotenuse, side — the one special case that does work."
        }
      ]
    },
    "trap": {
      "text": "Three facts are enough only when they are the right three facts."
    },
    "works": {
      "text": "SSS, SAS and ASA each include the information that fixes the triangle's shape."
    },
    "rhs": {
      "text": "The right angle removes the ambiguity — which is why RHS is a special case worth keeping."
    }
  },
  "why": "Congruence needs the right three facts: SSS, SAS, ASA (plus RHS) — and ASS is the famous near-miss."
};

const geoScaleEst = {
  "prompt": "A dog twice the size of a terrier (scale factor k = 2) is how many times as heavy?",
  "unit": "×",
  "answer": 8,
  "factor": 1.3,
  "why": "Volumes scale by k³ — 2³ = 8 — which is why elephants need thick legs while ants do not: weight grows as the cube, bone strength as the square.",
  "context": "lengths multiply by k, areas by k², volumes by k³"
};

const geoSymMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each figure with its symmetry:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "an isosceles triangle",
      "right": "one reflection axis"
    },
    {
      "left": "a square",
      "right": "four axes, rotational order 4"
    },
    {
      "left": "a circle",
      "right": "infinitely many axes"
    },
    {
      "left": "a regular pentagon",
      "right": "rotational order 5"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Symmetry is the deep principle that something survives a change — in the isosceles proof it is exactly why the base angles must match."
};

const geoTangentSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these circle theorems is misstated — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc.",
    "The angle in a semicircle is 90°.",
    "Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°.",
    "A tangent meets the radius at the point of contact at 45°."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact — 90°, not 45°.",
  "why": "The radius is the shortest line to the circle, so it must meet the tangent at 90°."
};

const geoCyclicOrder = {
  "prompt": "Rebuild the proof that opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180° — what comes first:",
  "items": [
    "Angle ADC subtends arc ABC",
    "The angle at the centre standing on that arc is twice angle ADC",
    "The reflex centre angle is twice angle ABC, which stands on the other arc",
    "ADC + ABC = half of 360° = 180°"
  ],
  "why": "Two applications of one theorem and an instant corollary — and the proof never measured anything. That is the payoff of the Euclidean method: many facts from one argument."
};

const geoNonEuclidSort = {
  "prompt": "File each claim under the non-Euclidean geometry it describes:",
  "buckets": [
    "hyperbolic (Lobachevsky, Bolyai)",
    "elliptic (Riemann)"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "infinitely many parallels through the point",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "no parallels at all",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "triangle angles sum to less than 180°",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "on a sphere, triangle angles can exceed 180°",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "The parallel postulate is not a truth of logic but a choice — and the universe we live in turns out to have made a non-Euclidean one, which GPS satellites correct for daily."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/euclidean-geometry">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Mathematics, on the foundational track. It assumes
    no prior geometry — we start with a piece of paper you can fold, tear and
    check with your own hands. Every new word is explained the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use geometry's basic vocabulary precisely — point, line, angle, parallel;</li>
    <li>state and use the core angle rules, from straight lines to polygons;</li>
    <li>decide when triangles are congruent or similar, and what each one guarantees;</li>
    <li>quote and apply the main circle theorems;</li>
    <li>follow a short proof — and explain why it beats measuring.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a paper triangle</h2>
  <p>
    Draw any triangle on a sheet of paper. Cut it out. Tear off its three corners
    and lay them edge to edge along a straight line: they fit together perfectly.
    Do it again with a completely different triangle — the same thing happens.
    The corners of any triangle fill a straight line, which is 180°.
  </p>
  <p>
    But how do you know it works for triangles you have never tried? Maybe
    tomorrow's triangle is the exception. Around 300 BCE in Alexandria, Euclid
    answered that worry with a book, the <em>Elements</em> — and with it invented
    a new kind of knowledge. The book does not begin with results. It begins with
    five <strong>axioms</strong> (Euclid called them postulates): innocent,
    self-evident statements such as "a straight line can be drawn between any two
    points" and "all right angles are equal". Everything else — the 465 theorems
    of the <em>Elements</em>, including results used in engineering, architecture
    and physics — is <strong>proven</strong>: each statement follows logically
    from the axioms and from earlier theorems, with every step visible and
    checkable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is believed on Euclid's authority; you can re-derive every claim
    yourself with a compass and a straightedge. Geometry is where humanity
    invented the idea that truth can be <em>earned</em>, step by visible step —
    the template every mathematical subject since has copied. And here is what a
    proof gives you that a measurement never could: it works for <em>every</em>
    triangle at once, including ones too small, too large or too skewed to draw.
    That leap from "the ones I checked" to "all of them" is what makes mathematics
    different from every empirical science.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="measurement or proof — file each guarantee" widget="sort-buckets" config={geoProofSort} />
  <Widget no="1" caption="a classic proof, honestly constructed — the isosceles triangle theorem" widget="equationStep" config={proofCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels and keep a tally of what
    each move is allowed to use: one given (AB = AC), one construction (the bisector AD),
    and one congruence rule (SAS). Watch the third panel pin the two half-triangles
    together — the shared side AD does the quiet work — and the fourth read off both equal
    base angles at once. No measurement enters anywhere: every step is earned from the one
    before it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch the proof above work. Two equal sides force two equal base angles, in
    four moves from nothing but one construction and one congruence rule. No
    protractor appears anywhere — and that is the point.
  </p>

  <h2>The words, and the rules of angles</h2>
  <p>
    Euclid first fixes the vocabulary, and precision here pays for itself. A
    <strong>point</strong> has position but no size. A <strong>line</strong> is
    infinitely long and perfectly straight. A <strong>plane</strong> is a flat
    surface. A <strong>segment</strong> is the finite piece between two points; a
    <strong>ray</strong> starts at a point and runs forever. An
    <strong>angle</strong> measures rotation between two rays, in degrees — a
    full turn is 360°, a convention inherited from Babylonian base-60 astronomy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The basic angle facts are few and mighty. Angles on a straight line sum to
    <strong>180°</strong>; angles around a point sum to <strong>360°</strong>;
    <strong>vertically opposite</strong> angles — the X made by two crossing
    lines — are equal. <strong>Complementary</strong> angles sum to 90°, like the
    two halves of a right angle; <strong>supplementary</strong> angles sum to
    180°.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each angle fact with its total" widget="match" config={geoAngleMatch} />
  <p>
    With <strong>parallel lines</strong> — lines in the same plane that never
    meet, however far you extend them — crossed by a third line called a
    <strong>transversal</strong>, three relationships do all the work:
    <strong>corresponding</strong> angles are equal (the F-shape),
    <strong>alternate</strong> angles are equal (the Z-shape), and
    <strong>co-interior</strong> angles sum to 180° (the C-shape).
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the blanks — the transversal's three relationships" widget="cloze" config={geoParallelCloze} />
  <p>
    From these, one theorem falls out immediately: the angles of any triangle sum
    to 180°. Draw a line through one corner parallel to the opposite side, and
    the triangle's three angles rearrange themselves into a straight line — the
    paper experiment, now earned. Push the same argument around an n-sided
    polygon and the interior angles sum to (n − 2) × 180°: 180° for a triangle,
    360° for a quadrilateral, 540° for a pentagon. Every fact here is proven, not
    asserted — which is why they hold everywhere, on every drawing, forever.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the hexagon's angle sum" widget="estimate" config={geoPolyEst} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a triangle's angles always sum to 180°." That is a
    theorem of flat planes, not of every surface: on a sphere, a triangle running from
    the equator to the North Pole and back can have three right angles — 270° in total —
    and on saddle-shaped surfaces the sum falls short of 180°. The theorem is exactly as
    true as the axiom about parallels it rests on, which is the Going deeper section's
    story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a proof is just a careful drawing." A diagram is a guide
    for the eye; the proof must not depend on how the diagram happens to look. The isosceles
    proof above works for triangles you could never draw accurately, and it never once
    measures anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a small set of angle facts — straight lines, points
    around a vertex, parallels cut by a transversal — earns the triangle sum and
    every polygon sum after it.
  </p>

  <h2>Congruence: when two figures are identical</h2>
  <p>
    Two figures are <strong>congruent</strong> if one can be placed exactly on the
    other by sliding, rotating or flipping — same size, same shape. The practical
    genius of Euclid is that you never need to check everything: triangles are
    pinned down by just three facts. <strong>SSS</strong> (three sides),
    <strong>SAS</strong> (two sides and the angle between them), <strong>ASA</strong>
    (two angles and the side between them) — each guarantees congruence, and this
    is why the proof above can stop after one side–angle–side comparison. (RHS —
    right angle, hypotenuse, side — is a special case; and <strong>ASS</strong>
    famously does <em>not</em> work: two different triangles can share two sides
    and a non-included angle.)
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="the ASS trap" widget="branch" config={geoAssBranch} />
  <p>
    Congruence is the engine of proof: to show two pieces of a figure are equal,
    carve the figure into congruent triangles — the move Euclid uses hundreds of
    times.
  </p>

  <h2>Similarity: same shape, different size</h2>
  <p>
    Two figures are <strong>similar</strong> if they have the same shape — equal
    matching angles and sides in the same ratio — without necessarily the same
    size. Every map is a similar copy of its territory; every architectural model
    is a similar copy of its building. The scale factor k drives a beautiful
    hierarchy: lengths multiply by k, <strong>areas by k²</strong>,
    <strong>volumes by k³</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That asymmetry explains things far outside geometry. A dog twice the size of
    a terrier is not twice as heavy-looking but eight times as heavy. A cake
    recipe for a 30 cm tin is not twice the recipe for a 15 cm tin but four times
    it. Elephants have thick legs while ants do not, because weight grows as the
    cube while bone strength grows as the square — giant versions of small
    animals run out of strength.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the weight of twice the size" widget="estimate" config={geoScaleEst} />
  <p>
    Similar triangles are also the surveyor's oldest tool. The ratio of shadow to
    height is the same for a stick and for a pyramid — which is how Thales
    reportedly measured the Great Pyramid around 600 BCE, and how you can find a
    river's width without crossing it. Matching angles make matching triangles,
    and matching triangles make unknown distances computable.
  </p>

  <h2>Symmetry: changes that leave things unchanged</h2>
  <p>
    A figure has <strong>line (reflectional) symmetry</strong> if some line folds
    it onto itself — an isosceles triangle has one such axis, a square four, a
    circle infinitely many. It has <strong>rotational symmetry</strong> of order n
    if it looks the same after each 1/n of a full turn — a square has order 4, a
    regular pentagon 5. Symmetry is more than prettiness: it is the deep
    principle that <em>something survives a change</em>. In the isosceles proof,
    reflection symmetry is exactly why the base angles must match; in physics,
    conservation laws arise from symmetries of space and time (Noether's
    theorem); in chemistry, molecular symmetry predicts spectra. Learning to
    <em>see</em> symmetry here is the first training in using it as a tool.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each figure with its symmetry" widget="match" config={geoSymMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> congruence pins figures down with three facts and
    powers proofs; similarity scales shapes and their areas and volumes by k, k²,
    k³; symmetry says what survives a change.
  </p>

  <h2>Circle theorems: the crown of school geometry</h2>
  <p>
    Circles generate the most famous theorem-pack in mathematics, and each result
    follows from the same move: comparing an angle <strong>subtended</strong> —
    propped open — at the circumference with the same arc seen from the centre.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "π equals 22/7." That is a handy approximation,
    accurate only to two decimal places (3.142857… against 3.141592…), and nothing more:
    π is irrational — proven by Lambert in 1761 — so no fraction of integers equals it,
    and no calculator will ever write it out. Use 22/7 for estimation by all means; just
    never call it exact.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference</strong> standing on the same arc. Every other result is a corollary.</li>
    <li><strong>The angle in a semicircle is 90°</strong> — the centre-angle is 180°, so any triangle inscribed with the diameter as one side is right-angled (Thales' theorem).</li>
    <li><strong>Angles in the same segment are equal</strong> — every point on the same arc sees the chord under the same angle; that is why inscribed angles "sweep" equally.</li>
    <li><strong>Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°</strong> — the four corners of any quadrilateral inscribed in a circle.</li>
    <li><strong>A tangent is perpendicular to the radius</strong> at the point of contact — the radius is the shortest line to the circle, so it must meet the tangent at 90°.</li>
    <li><strong>The alternate segment theorem:</strong> the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle the chord subtends in the opposite segment — the fact behind how sound and light "leave" curved surfaces.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worked taste of the method: take a triangle inscribed in a circle with one
    side the diameter. The angle at the centre, standing on that diameter, is a
    straight 180°. The centre-angle theorem says the angle at the circumference
    is half of it — 90°. No measurement, no protractor: the right angle is earned
    in two lines, and it holds for <em>every</em> point on the semicircle at
    once. That is the payoff of the Euclidean method — many facts from one
    argument.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the backwards theorem" widget="spot-error" config={geoTangentSpot} />
  <p>
    One more proof shows the theorems combining. Take a quadrilateral inscribed
    in a circle (a <strong>cyclic quadrilateral</strong>) and ask why its opposite
    angles sum to 180°. Label the vertices A, B, C, D and let angle ADC subtend
    arc ABC. The angle at the centre standing on that same arc is twice angle ADC
    — and the <em>reflex</em> angle at the centre (the rest of the full 360°) is
    twice angle ABC, since angle ABC stands on the other arc. So angle ADC +
    angle ABC = half of 360° = 180°. Two applications of one theorem, an instant
    corollary — and notice the proof never measured anything. This is also how
    exam problems here are designed: almost every "find the angle" puzzle is a
    two-step chain through these six results, and the skill being trained is
    choosing which theorem links the angles you have to the angle you want.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the cyclic-quadrilateral proof" widget="order" config={geoCyclicOrder} />
  <p>
    One numerical workout ties the theorems together. Inscribe a triangle in a
    semicircle whose diameter is 10, with one leg 6. Thales' theorem hands over
    the right angle for free; Pythagoras gives the third side: √(100 − 36) = 8 —
    a 6-8-10 triangle, from any point on the arc whatsoever. Now drop the
    perpendicular from the right angle to the diameter: the foot divides the
    diameter into 3.6 and 6.4, and the altitude is √(3.6 × 6.4) = 4.8. Two
    theorems, one figure, no protractor — the chain of earned results is the
    whole method.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, and one angle is 65°. What is its co-interior partner, and what is its alternate partner?</li>
    <li>An isosceles triangle has an apex angle of 40°. Find both base angles — without measuring.</li>
    <li>A triangle is inscribed in a semicircle of diameter 26, and one leg is 10. Find the third side.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Co-interior angles sum to 180°, so the partner
    is 180° − 65° = 115°; alternate angles are equal, so that one is 65°.
    (2) The angles sum to 180°, so the two base angles together are 180° − 40° =
    140°; the triangle is isosceles, so each is 140° ÷ 2 = 70°. (3) Thales'
    theorem makes it right-angled, so Pythagoras applies: √(26² − 10²) = √(676 −
    100) = √576 = 24.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Euclid's method: a few axioms, then every claim proven step by visible step — truth earned, not asserted.</li>
    <li>Angles on a straight line sum to 180°, angles around a point to 360°, and vertically opposite angles are equal.</li>
    <li>Parallel lines cut by a transversal: corresponding and alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles sum to 180°.</li>
    <li>Triangles are pinned down by three facts — SSS, SAS, ASA (plus RHS) — which lets proofs carve figures into congruent pieces.</li>
    <li>Similarity scales lengths by k, areas by k², volumes by k³ — why giant animals need thick legs and cake recipes do not simply double.</li>
    <li>The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference, and the angle in a semicircle is 90°.</li>
    <li>A proof covers every triangle at once, including ones too small, too large or too skewed to draw.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The theorems earned here go straight to work in the next lessons on this track:
    perimeter, area and volume puts similarity's k, k², k³ scaling to work on measures, and
    quadratics borrows the right angles that Thales' theorem hands out for free. And when a
    later course needs a certainty no measurement can supply, it will borrow Euclid's method
    itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The five axioms deserve their own scrutiny, because four of them behave and the fifth
    doesn't. Axioms 1–4 (draw a line; extend a line; draw a circle; all right angles are
    equal) feel airtight. The fifth — the <strong>parallel postulate</strong>, that through a
    point off a line there is exactly one parallel line — feels like it should be provable
    from the others, and for twenty centuries mathematicians tried. The failures ended in the
    1820s–40s when Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Gauss built <strong>hyperbolic geometry</strong>
    (infinitely many parallels, triangles whose angles sum to <em>less</em> than 180°) and
    Riemann built <strong>elliptic geometry</strong> (no parallels; on a sphere, triangles can
    have angles summing to more than 180° — fly a plane along a great-circle triangle and
    check). Both are fully consistent: if Euclid's geometry contains a contradiction, so do
    they, and vice versa. The parallel postulate is not a truth of logic but a
    <em>choice</em>, and the universe we live in turns out to have made a non-Euclidean one —
    Einstein's general relativity describes gravity as spacetime curvature, geometry in the
    Riemannian family. GPS satellites correct for it daily.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="hyperbolic or elliptic — file each claim" widget="sort-buckets" config={geoNonEuclidSort} />
  <p>
    Two deeper footnotes. First, Euclid's axioms had quiet gaps — he assumed, without saying,
    that a line has points between any two of its points — and it took David Hilbert in 1899
    to supply a complete, airtight axiom set for plane geometry, closing the subject's
    foundations twenty-three centuries later. Second, the straightedge-and-compass tradition
    is secretly about <strong>constructible numbers</strong>: with compass and straightedge you
    can bisect any angle, construct any rational multiple, and solve quadratics — but trisect
    an arbitrary angle, square the circle or double the cube? Proven impossible in the 1800s,
    by showing each construction would force algebra (specifically, degree-2 field extensions
    and, for the circle, the transcendence of π) that no finite chain of compass moves can
    reach. Even the limits of the method were finally proved — by the method's own descendants.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Euclid, Elements (c. 300 BCE); Heath's translation and commentary; Greenberg, Euclidean and Non-Euclidean Geometries; Hilbert, Foundations of Geometry (1899). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
